{"id":108164,"title":"Hierarchical Volume Representation with 3{radical}2 Subdivision and Trivariate B-Spline Wavelets","url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/?q=multiresolution volume rendering","final_url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/m2/1/high_res_d/15006860.pdf","year":null,"kind":"link","source":"os:digital.library.unt.edu","doi":null,"arxiv":null,"sha256":"6452d75e182b671d0d5054d7dbfc5e7732740c24aba430413a422d8210f90a33","ctype":"application/pdf","text_src":"url","fetched_at":1793234910.1719403,"text_chars":40210,"abstract":"Multiresolution methods provide a means for representing data at multiple levels of detail. They are typically based on a hierarchical data organization scheme and update rules needed for data value computation. We use a data organization that is based on what we call n{radical}2 subdivision. The main advantage of subdivision, compared to quadtree (n = 2) or octree (n = 3) organizations, is that the number of vertices is only doubled in each subdivision step instead of multiplied by a factor of four or eight, respectively. To update data values we use n-variate B-spline wavelets, which yields better approximations for each level of detail. We develop a lifting scheme for n = 2 and n = 3 based on the n{radical}2-subdivision scheme. We obtain narrow masks that could also provide a basis for view-dependent visualization and adaptive refinement.","file":null,"marks":["unofficial"],"links":["https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/m2/1/high_res_d/15006860.pdf","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/?q=multiresolution volume rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/?q=adaptive mesh refinement visualization ray casting","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/?q=tetrahedral mesh volume rendering","https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1411654/?q=curvilinear grid volume rendering","https://digital.libra